{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"FULL-SUITE RESULT + a real defect the suite caught in my own test (patchset 2 of Gerrit 1963).\n\nRan the complete default tier locally: `pytest -m \"not integration and not external\" -n 4\n--dist worksteal` -> **14821 passed, 55 skipped, 1 xfailed, 1 failed, 4 errors** in 18m24s.\nReporting the non-green results rather than rounding them off, because one of them was mine.\n\n## The 1 failure was REAL and it was my new test's fault\n\n`tests/unit/test_subprocess_env_repr_security.py::test_all_non_fixture_subprocess_environments_use_the_safe_boundary`\nfailed, naming `tests/unit/test_live_jira_xdist_confinement.py:143` — the held-out test I wrote.\nIts subprocess helper built the child environment as a plain `dict(os.environ)`. This repo\nforbids that: pytest prints call arguments in its default long traceback, so a plain dict dumps\nevery inherited environment value if subprocess startup ever fails. The boundary is\n`tests/_subprocess_env.py`'s `subprocess_env()`, whose `SubprocessEnv.__repr__` redacts.\n\nThat policy is pointed squarely at this test. It injects fake `JIRA_*` values, and it runs on\nhosts that carry REAL Jira credentials — this one does. A startup failure would have printed\nthem. Fixed in patchset 2: `env = subprocess_env()`, with a comment recording why.\n\nWorth stating plainly: `make lint`, `make typecheck` and the complexity ratchet were all green\nacross this the whole time. Only the broader suite caught it. This is exactly the case for not\ntreating the fast gates as sufficient — and I would have shipped it had I stopped at them.\n\n## The 4 errors were self-inflicted, not regressions\n\nAll four were `Failed: Test moved the repo HEAD (987caf8c73 -> a13190d82c)`. I committed the fix\nWHILE the suite was running, so tests that assert the repo HEAD is stable saw it move underneath\nthem. My own process artifact, not a defect in the change. Confirmed by re-running all four with\nHEAD stable: **4 passed**. The lesson is procedural (don't commit mid-suite), and I am recording\nit rather than quietly re-running until green — per this project's rule that a retry is not a\ndiagnosis.\n\n## Re-verification after the patchset-2 edit\n\nBecause I touched the held-out test, I re-ran its teeth rather than assuming they survived. All\nthree defect-seeded mutations still return it to RED, each on a different assertion:\n- re-remove the `xdist_group` decorator -> the AST policy scan goes RED;\n- neuter the loadgroup check -> the behavioural test goes RED;\n- neuter the credentials check -> the CI-safety no-op test goes RED.\nThe oracle itself: 5 passed. `test_subprocess_env_repr_security.py`: 20 passed. Gates re-run\ngreen (`lint`, `typecheck`, `active=49 new=0 increased=0 stale=0`).\n\nPatchset 2 carries the same Change-Id (I2658b614...), so it is a new patchset on 1963, not a\nduplicate change. `LLM-Review +1` was cast on patchset 1; both votes will re-run against\npatchset 2, and the ticket stays in_progress until `Verified +1`."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787261267887321001,"uuid":"a5532b5e-ac4a-46fa-9215-de81bd2870c7"}